WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.140
At this software company,

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:06.730
pay negotiations are conducted
a little differently.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.060
Here it's your colleague
that sets your salary.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:10.893
Because we trust them,

00:00:10.893 --> 00:00:13.981
we should have transparency
that will be paid back

00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:17.941
by more trust from the people
in the team, the company.

00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:21.140
And that will create a
positive feedback loop

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.118
that will benefit everyone.

00:00:23.118 --> 00:00:25.350
Buenos Aires based 10Pines,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.350
has an open book management style

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.710
that allows employees to have
a say in all major decisions,

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.070
including who they hire
and how much they're paid.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.770
In the final interview
of the hiring process,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:42.410
the candidate meets the entire
company, around 90 people,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.860
who in turn decide if they're a good fit

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.870
and then their starting salary.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.300
It's important that if
this person, new hire,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.500
enters the company,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.210
then everyone will know the salary

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.560
and will accept and will acknowledge

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.220
that it looks at least,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.240
with a small information that
we have from that person,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.370
it looks fair at that time.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:03.350
Three times a year

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.750
the company has a rates meeting

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.020
in which employees can pitch and present

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.870
why they deserve a raise

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.590
before their teammates take a vote.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.370
We live in a market that
it's competing all the time

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.320
for the talent.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.900
And of course, one of the
ways that it compete is

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.650
by paying and raising
salaries all the time.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.210
So it's an important aspect
that people needs to know

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.360
if they are being paid
a fair salary or not.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.300
10Pines calls this sociocracy.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.550
And although it may sound unique,

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.370
its practices are
grounded in a larger trend

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.830
known as radical transparency.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.680
Right now we're really fertile ground

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.610
for this kind of idea to sprout up.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.443
And I think we're going to see

00:01:42.443 --> 00:01:45.010
many, many, many things kind of popping up

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:45.843
all over the world.

00:01:45.843 --> 00:01:48.120
Many, many experiments over
the next couple of years

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.880
in this approach.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.880
And so, as companies and employees

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.630
start to look towards a
post pandemic workplace,

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.343
could honesty really be the best policy?

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.190
Radical transparency is a
concept that broadly applied

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.500
just means being super transparent

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.770
about details that a
company or an organization

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.580
might otherwise choose to
keep secret or keep hidden

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.420
or limited to a certain
number of executives

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.743
or leaders at a company.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.560
Netflix is one of the companies

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.110
best known for its culture

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.120
of radical transparency

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.593
that it says promotes accountability.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.380
Netflix, the company, takes
radical transparency very far

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.380
and they go so far as to have, you know,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.710
public discussions or public
emails sent out to, you know,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:42.900
sometimes hundreds of employees

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.240
discussing why someone was fired.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.550
Sometimes the discussions about
why someone is getting fired

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.100
happen right in front of them,

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.110
so that they have a chance
to share all the ways

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.440
that they made mistakes

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.983
that led to them no longer
working at the company.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.210
But they also have policies

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.450
that people might see as pretty welcome.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.410
So for example,

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:06.030
they allow their employees to
sort of, at their discretion,

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.630
make use of their expense accounts.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.390
So that means no one's really
tracking their vacation days

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.100
or their expense spending

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.410
or things that are you know,

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.610
useful to this person
getting their job done.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.710
And the idea is that it's freedom

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:20.940
and sort of freedom and responsibility

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.580
that is given to these employees

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.700
so that they have a chance to
do their job the best they can

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.533
without being micromanaged.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.260
But you only have to look at Netflix

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.293
on Glassdoor to see that this
approach isn't for everyone.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.380
Anytime you have a company culture

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.730
that's sort of extreme,

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:39.970
you have the chance to attract people

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.110
for whom it's really a good fit.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.640
And you're also going to
turn off a lot of people

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.330
who just feel that this isn't for them.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:47.310
And it may explain why

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.450
this isn't a more widely adopted thing

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.430
because it's difficult

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.920
and it requires someone at
the top, really committed,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.450
to working through the
uncomfortable parts of it

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.633
in order to make it the company culture.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:03:59.620
For Netflix,

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.610
that person at the top is Reed Hastings.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.750
He believes the company's success

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.810
stems from its high performance culture.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.610
In the traditional industrial
paradigm, you know,

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.460
you have to do something
wrong to get let go.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.360
You can think of a job as
sort of a property right.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.880
You know, until you lose it
by abusing your position.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.950
But if you think about
professional sports, you know,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.720
if that team is going
to win a championship,

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.910
it has to have a mix of the right players

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:29.400
that work well together,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.980
that are the absolute best in the world.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.930
And so we try to model ourselves

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.700
like a professional sports team.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.370
So highly paid,

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:40.230
but you got to earn your
position every year.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.790
And it's about performance.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.950
And you know, that's
not right for everybody.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.710
Some people care mostly
about job security.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.380
Other people care mostly
about excellent colleagues

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.560
and you know, playing great team ball

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.040
to achieving something
important for the consumers.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.510
And the Netflix numbers

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:03.730
seem to show that radical
transparency is working,

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:06.240
while it fires people at a higher rate

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.290
than the average US company.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.663
People actually choose to
leave at a much lower rate.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.700
But you also don't hear
about the companies

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.360
for which this didn't work so well, right.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.370
Or the companies that might
have gotten to midsize

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.500
and then things sort of fizzled out.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.970
Or you know, we look at
Netflix and Bridgewater

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.710
and we see these success stories,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.400
but we're also not hearing
necessarily the stories

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.240
where it was hard to keep
a company culture going

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.073
as it grew much bigger

00:05:32.073 --> 00:05:33.580
or it was hard to keep it going

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.463
as the company expanded very quickly.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.490
But beyond company success,

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.950
how does this high accountability system

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.380
impact the average employee?

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.760
So from a neuroscience perspective,

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.410
if we think about what the
brain is innately craving,

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.810
like the brain's own goals,

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.820
one of them is a sense of certainty.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.870
Like the ability to predict

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.890
what's going to happen the next second.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.800
When you feel, you know, uncertain,

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.730
it activates quite a strong
threat response in the brain,

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.610
which takes your attention away from

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:08.110
what you're trying to focus on.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.700
When certainty goes up,

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.950
you activate a reward network in the brain

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.640
and your field of view literally opens up

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.480
your perspective and how much
you can think about opens up

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.560
and your ability to be creative.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.090
So one of the things with
radical transparency is

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.260
people have a lot more certainty

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.450
about what's expected of them,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.470
about what great performance
looks like in any domain,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.740
about what other people are doing,

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.430
how that compares to what they're doing.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.300
So it really increases
your access to information.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.600
So you can feel a lot more certain

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.950
about everything that's
going on in your world.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.700
And this drive for certainty

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.850
is such a fundamental part of the brain.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.370
It's literally one of the
brain's intrinsic goals.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.780
And it's not just

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:53.000
an increased sense of certainty

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.030
that could help us do our jobs.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.990
When you give people
access to information about

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.700
who's paid what and how

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.800
and what they're doing and
how the whole business works

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:04.920
and the whole infrastructure,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.630
people feel a much
greater sense of control.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:10.110
They feel like they can make decisions.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.570
They feel like they are
able to to take actions

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:14.740
and as a result,

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.840
improve their career or their performance.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.420
While a radical transparency model

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.680
may check out on a biological level.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:28.810
It definitely isn't a one
size fits all solution.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.100
As humans we have incredible
neural diversity, right?

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.330
There are people who thrive
under incredible pressure.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.070
And in fact, they're really bored

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.870
unless there's enormous change
and uncertainty going on.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.940
And there are people who will fall apart

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.690
if the subway schedule changes.

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.850
I will say that radical
transparency as a concept,

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.690
will suit a number of
kinds of organizations

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.410
or maybe even functions.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.330
It doesn't mean necessarily
it will suit every person

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.540
or even necessarily every organization.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:00.373
It's interesting because

00:08:00.373 --> 00:08:01.510
I think there are certain companies

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.250
that have been pushing really
hard for radical transparency

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.120
for a long time, for a decade plus.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.370
And it hasn't had quite
this moment of, you know,

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:12.530
a moment in time in which
it became very popular.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.520
I think that's because
it's probably quite hard

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:16.353
to adjust to

00:08:16.353 --> 00:08:18.300
and so you see companies

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.720
who really define themselves
as a company culture,

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.990
try to differentiate
themselves from other companies

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:25.580
by emphasizing radical transparency.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.170
But those companies in my view,

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:28.963
remain pretty few and far between.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.090
In addition to the potential legal issues

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:33.850
surrounding employee performance

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.130
and termination transparency,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.963
a major hurdle for many companies
is making salaries public.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.770
Yep. It's 2021.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:44.750
And women are still
being paid less than men

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.680
for doing the same jobs.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:50.295
One step in the direction of

00:08:50.295 --> 00:08:53.760
amending this reality

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.980
is to have pay transparency.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.120
In the case of pay transparency,

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.290
there's this pretty strong argument that

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.870
actually hiding pay between your employees

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.380
can hold people back.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.570
It lets certain employees get paid more

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:10.370
and certain employees go
underpaid for a long time

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:11.340
without knowing.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:12.350
And so there's, I think,

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.406
a pretty strong argument to be made that,

00:09:15.406 --> 00:09:17.460
you know, that pay
transparency's more fair.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.660
A 2021 Washington University study

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.730
found that pay secrecy
directly contributes

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.040
to the continued pay inequality

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.260
between men and women doing the same role.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.200
I wouldn't be surprised

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.800
if certain of the more palatable
or easy to digest changes

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.190
that come with radical transparency,

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:39.070
say maybe like pay transparency.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.300
I think that's something
that more companies

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.420
could get on board with adopting.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.920
It's less of a culture shock than

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.210
changing the way that you
do all sorts of feedback

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:51.060
or firings or meetings or
sensitive things like that.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.230
And if that becomes more and more common,

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:57.020
then you'll see companies who
want to distinguish themselves

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:58.630
thinking about, well, what's next?

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.830
Like what is something else
that we can be changing

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:01.960
about how we run our business,

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.370
that's going to make
us better and different

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:05.743
from our competitors?

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:08.670
A year of remote working

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:10.680
has challenged us to rethink

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:13.283
many of our traditional work models.

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:18.000
And while radical transparency,
in its most extreme form,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.860
may not become the norm,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:23.220
some of it's central
practices like flexibility,

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.000
employee autonomy and openness,

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.430
maybe on the way.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:29.600
What the pandemic, in
the last year, has done

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.820
is it's kind of given a lot of people

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:36.450
a lot more clarity about
what matters to them

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.440
and a lot more willingness to speak up

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.310
and also vote with their feet.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.020
And I think what we're
going to see as a result

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.030
is what might've taken kind of
five to ten years to happen,

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:48.310
I think it's going to
happen in one to two,

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:50.530
in terms of organizational culture.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:51.980
I don't necessarily predict a revolution,

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.860
but I predict some
revolutionary approaches

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.095
are going to start taking
hold in organizations

00:10:58.095 --> 00:10:59.650
in the next couple of years.

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:02.060
We're seeing that in a
really short amount of time,

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:03.700
things changed so dramatically.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:05.640
And for some organizations, for example,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.670
it was impossible to think like

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:09.390
people working from their home all day.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:10.510
And from one day to the other,

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:12.720
we're forced to move to that situation.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:15.450
And it just so that people keep working

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:16.670
and things keep moving

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:19.123
and it's possible to do such things.

